cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "yachay — null-calibrated shortlisting, and an atlas of where the evidence stops"
message: "If you use this work, cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Yuri
    family-names: Freitas
keywords:
  - selection bias
  - order statistics
  - winner's curse
  - null calibration
  - CRISPR screen
  - DepMap
  - target prioritisation
  - best-of-N
  - rare disease
  - founder effect
  - ancestry representation
  - health equity
license: CC-BY-4.0
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-26"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The claims this repository stands behind, each with the number that supports it.
# Genealogy in docs/lineage.md; references in docs/references/; stages in docs/methodology.md.
#
#  · the celebrated "training maximum" of the obesity screen (2.0047) was measured on ONE
#    observation, where pure noise averages 0.845 and reaches 2.43 at p99 — the record
#    score sat at roughly the 93rd percentile of NOTHING HAPPENING
#  · a -0.57 correlation with observation count, diagnosed as a viability confound,
#    fell to +0.07 after calibration: the biology was a statistical artifact
#  · the single most important entity in that screen moved from rank 12 to rank 1
#  · at DepMap scale (1178 lines x 17916 genes): 60% of the raw top 10 are known
#    PAN-ESSENTIAL genes — the metric's maximum is toxicity, not selectivity
#  · the same 1178 lines, grouped by Oncotree instead of pooled, recover textbook lineage
#    dependencies BLIND — SOX10 and BRAF in skin, TCF7L2 and CTNNB1 in bowel, ISL1/LDB1/
#    GATA3/MYCN in peripheral nervous system, PAX8 in kidney/ovary/uterus, FLI1 in bone;
#    5 of 9 pre-named positive controls returned with a rank, and the other 4 are published
#  · pooled "Lung" (126 lines, the best-powered subgroup) returns NOTHING, while its two
#    subtypes each return the genes their biology predicts (KRAS; SKP2/CKS1B/E2F3) — a null
#    result at the coarse level can be evidence of the wrong grouping, not of no effect
#  · 27 of 37 subtypes cannot detect a 0.8 SD effect and are reported as underpowered
#    rather than as negative
#  · grouping the same lines by GENOTYPE instead of by catalogue label reproduces paralog
#    synthetic lethality under lineage stratification (SMARCA4->SMARCA2 0.97->0.93;
#    ARID1A->ARID1B 0.88->0.96, where stratifying made the effect LARGER because lineage
#    was masking it) and the loss of a dependency (TP53->MDM2 -1.58, unmoved)
#  · but 92 of 121 mutation-defined subgroups are substantially restating MUTATIONAL
#    BURDEN rather than genotype - hypermutated lines are mutated in everything, so a long
#    gene becomes a synonym for hypermutation; this is the pan-essential confound one
#    level up, and it CANNOT be adjusted away, because for a mismatch-repair gene the same
#    separation is the mechanism (MSH3->WRN falls 1.69->0.82 when it is)
#  · the damaging-mutation matrix cannot express an activating hotspot, so BRAF shows 9
#    mutant lines against the ~100 carrying V600E: half the pre-written control set was
#    inconstructible, which is a result about the data source and is published as one
#  · with the gates exposed as live controls, 26 of 40 Skin candidates fail on the Stage 0
#    dependency floor ALONE and the false-discovery and effect-size cuts exclude nothing
#    between them - two conventional thresholds doing no work on this data
#  · the measured null bias of a top-20 mean runs 0.3934 (n=347) -> 0.5638 (n=1178)
#  · the two anomalies this file used to list as OPEN are RESOLVED (2026-08-26), and both
#    were defects in the same estimator: the null was fitted on pooled control ROWS, so it
#    described a synthetic entity rather than a gene. Fitting on gene-shaped BLOCKS moves
#    the nonessential control pool from a mean z of -4.09 to +0.036 (sd 1.013), and the
#    count correlation from -0.0559 to -0.0377. The count-correlation move was settled with
#    this repository's first confidence interval: 4,000-resample paired bootstrap over
#    17,916 genes, change in Spearman 95% CI [-0.0344, -0.0271], P(>0)=0.000 - a defect,
#    not noise. Evidence in docs/references/deep/internal-audit.md; predicted in advance by
#    docs/adr/0004-block-nulls.md and scored correct.
#  · the population axis, measured 2026-08-27 over Orphanet's 17,108 prevalence records:
#    386 of 525 (73.5%) disorders reported in more than one country fall into DIFFERENT
#    prevalence classes by country, so prevalence is not a scalar; 55.6% of records assert
#    'Worldwide', a claim of population-independence that is false wherever it is testable;
#    representation against population runs Europe 8.10 to Africa 0.07, a factor of 116;
#    and the catalogue's entire vocabulary for a population is one untyped string
#  · ONE OPEN ANOMALY remains: the nonessential control set may not be inert. Its 99.95th
#    percentile of z sits at 8.9 where a standard normal gives 3.3 - a control-set PURITY
#    problem, not a null problem. Not fixed, not diagnosed (docs/lineage.md §10).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

references:
  # --- the author's own prior work: where the method and the discipline come from ----
  - type: software
    title: "nominator — turn sparse, confounded assay data into a defensible shortlist"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    license: Apache-2.0
    notes: >-
      THE DIRECT METHODOLOGICAL ANCESTOR, and uncredited here until 2026-08-28. Nine of this
      repository's ten stages are nominator's, renumbered by the insertion of Stage 1 (Null),
      which is what sieve genuinely contributes and what produced every headline result in
      this file. nominator implements all ten stages in 921 lines; sieve implements four in
      1,627. Its core/validation.py already carried `bootstrap_ci`, `leave_one_entity_out`,
      `cold_start_split` and `orthogonal_validation` - so finding A6 ("no intervals on
      published numbers") stayed open here for fourteen audit sweeps and was closed by
      rewriting a bootstrap the ancestor shipped. See docs/references/prior-work.md and
      docs/lineage.md §13.
  - type: software
    title: "climate — regime engine for Rio Grande do Sul"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    notes: >-
      States Stage 2 as the constraint that governs the architecture rather than as a gate:
      with n=36 and SE(RPSS) ~ 0.10-0.15 the standard error is the order of the whole signal,
      therefore no empirical arbitrage between models exists. sieve reached the same
      conclusion for rare-disease evidence on 2026-08-28 (audit A26) and acted on it less
      decisively.
  - type: software
    title: "adia — real-time structural break detection (ADIA Lab / CrunchDAO 2026)"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    notes: >-
      Carries the number Stage 5 exists to produce and sieve has never produced: a measured
      optimism gap of ~ +0.013 between a local holdout (~0.60) and the board (0.5910 TS-AUC),
      with the stated consequence "regularize and prune, do not add".
  - type: software
    title: "knee — report-derived multilingual labelling and abnormality detection in knee MRI"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      Source of the documentation discipline this repository adopts: a role/last-revised/state
      header on every doc, a lineage file that states what each measurement DOES to the
      ancestor's claim, and an archive of dead ends with the number that killed each. Its
      teacher-student inversion (CV 0.7652 vs 0.787 against humans) is the same lesson as
      Stage 5 here: an in-metric validation number is not a held-out number.
  - type: software
    title: "agentComp — hybrid agent (LLM + causal engine) for ARC-AGI-3"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      Origin of the methodological laws inherited through `knee`, in particular "a wrong
      premise costs more than wrong code" — which is Stage 0 of this library.
  - type: software
    title: "prompt-workbench (Oráculo) — prompt and model evaluation harness"
    authors:
      - { given-names: Yuri, family-names: Freitas }
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      The second adapter (`sieve.adapters.llm_eval`). Building it exposed a design flaw in
      the core (reduce="mean" vs reduce="raw"), which is the return on porting a method to a
      domain that did not produce it.

  # --- the mechanism: why a maximum is biased ---------------------------------------
  - type: book
    title: "Order Statistics"
    authors:
      - { family-names: David, given-names: "H. A." }
      - { family-names: Nagaraja, given-names: "H. N." }
    year: 2003
    notes: >-
      The mechanism underneath every stage-1 claim: E[max of n draws] grows with n. Stage 1
      is this fact, measured empirically from the screen's own controls instead of assumed
      from a distributional form.
  - type: article
    title: "Extreme value theory / the expected maximum of noise"
    notes: >-
      Placeholder for the specific EVT reference to be chosen. What matters for this library
      is the NON-asymptotic regime — n=1 to n=20 — where EVT's limits do not apply and the
      empirical null does. To be filled with the reference that actually covers small n.

  # --- the same bias, once per field, each with its own name -------------------------
  - type: article
    title: "Winner's curse in genome-wide association studies / effect-size inflation at discovered loci"
    notes: >-
      The genetics name for Stage 1. The effect at a locus discovered by its own significance
      is biased upward, and the bias grows as power falls. Directly relevant to the planned
      GWAS adapter (docs/disease-expansion.md §2).
  - type: article
    title: "The Deflated Sharpe Ratio: Correcting for Selection Bias, Backtest Overfitting and Non-Normality"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Bailey, given-names: "David H." }
      - { family-names: "López de Prado", given-names: Marcos }
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      The finance name for Stage 1, and the closest prior art in spirit: it deflates a
      selected maximum by the number of trials. Difference worth stating plainly — the DSR
      deflates from a formula's assumptions; `sieve` resamples the screen's own controls,
      so it inherits the real correlation structure rather than assuming independence.
  - type: article
    title: "The Probability of Backtest Overfitting"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Bailey, given-names: "David H." }
      - { family-names: "López de Prado", given-names: Marcos }
    notes: "Companion to the above; the out-of-sample degradation of a max-selected strategy."
  - type: article
    title: "Limit theorems for the maximum term in stationary sequences (Darling–Erdős)"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Darling, given-names: "D. A." }
      - { family-names: "Erdős", given-names: "P." }
    year: 1956
    notes: >-
      The change-point name for Stage 1, and the designated ANALYTIC CHECK: scanning
      positions and taking the largest statistic has a known correction. `sieve` should
      reproduce it empirically. If it cannot, the library is wrong — see
      docs/expansion-map.md §2.
  - type: article
    title: "Regression to the mean"
    notes: "The clinical-trial name for the same thing. Kept in the list so the shared mechanism is visible."

  # --- gene-based tests: the correction the GWAS adapter must reproduce ---------------
  - type: article
    title: "MAGMA: Generalized Gene-Set Analysis of GWAS Data"
    authors:
      - { family-names: "de Leeuw", given-names: "Christiaan A." }
      - { family-names: Posthuma, given-names: Danielle }
    year: 2015
    notes: >-
      A gene score built as a max/top-k over the SNPs in a gene is inflated by how many
      correlated SNPs the gene contains. MAGMA corrects this parametrically. The
      schizophrenia adapter's acceptance criterion is agreement with it.
  - type: article
    title: "VEGAS: gene-based test for genome-wide association studies"
    notes: "Simulation-based gene test using an LD reference — closest in method to what `sieve` would do."
  - type: article
    title: "Controlling the False Discovery Rate: a Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Benjamini, given-names: Yoav }
      - { family-names: Hochberg, given-names: Yosef }
    year: 1995
    notes: >-
      Stage 6's error control. Stated here because it is the standard fix that does NOT
      solve the Stage 1 problem: FDR controls the rate of false positives among tests, but
      the ranking within survivors is still a ranking of observation count.

  # --- the reference dataset -----------------------------------------------------------
  - type: database
    title: "DepMap — Cancer Dependency Map, CRISPR gene effect (Achilles / Project Score)"
    notes: >-
      The scale test: 1178 cell lines x 17916 genes as run in out/DEPMAP_FINDINGS.md.
      Supplies both control sets used here — AchillesNonessentialControls (the Stage 1 null
      pool) and AchillesCommonEssentialControls (the Stage 3 confound). Release version to
      be pinned in docs/references/README.md.
  - type: article
    title: "Chronos: a cell population dynamics model of CRISPR experiments (gene effect estimation)"
    notes: >-
      Defines the score this library consumes: 0 = no effect, -1 = the median common-essential
      gene, dependency NEGATIVE — which is why the adapter flips the sign.
  - type: article
    title: "Defining a Cancer Dependency Map"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Tsherniak, given-names: Aviad }
      - { family-names: Hahn, given-names: "William C." }
    year: 2017
    notes: "The framing of selective dependency as the useful signal, and pan-essentiality as the confound."

  # --- NF2: the focused disease reference ---------------------------------------------
  - type: article
    title: "Updated diagnostic criteria and nomenclature for neurofibromatosis type 2 and schwannomatosis (2022 consensus)"
    year: 2022
    notes: >-
      The rename to NF2-related schwannomatosis, and the separation from SMARCB1/LZTR1
      schwannomatosis. Supports docs/references/nf2.md §1 — the section that exists because
      the wrong name returns the wrong literature.
  - type: article
    title: "The NF2 tumour suppressor merlin and the Hippo pathway (review — reference to be selected)"
    notes: >-
      Supports nf2.md §3: merlin acts upstream of LATS1/2, and merlin loss releases YAP/TAZ
      to act with TEAD. This is the claim that turns into a POSITIVE CONTROL for the NF2
      analysis — if the calibrated ranking does not recover YAP1/WWTR1/TEAD1-4/LATS1-2, the
      pipeline is broken and no novel hit from it should be believed.
  - type: article
    title: "NF2 loss and YAP/TAZ–TEAD dependency in mesothelioma (primary reports — to be selected)"
    notes: >-
      Supports nf2.md §5, and also names the main threat to that analysis: mesothelioma is
      over-represented among NF2-null lines, so a lineage effect can masquerade as an NF2
      effect unless lineage is regressed out at Stage 3.
  - type: article
    title: "Bevacizumab for vestibular schwannoma in NF2 — hearing response"
    notes: "Supports nf2.md §4: the endpoint is hearing preservation in a benign tumour, not cytotoxicity."
  - type: article
    title: "TEAD inhibitors — clinical status (to be selected)"
    notes: "The pharmacological expression of the Hippo logic; the most mechanistically motivated current class."

  # --- rare-disease scale: the quantitative axis (docs/references/rare-disease-scale.md) --
  # Every entry in the three blocks below was resolved through the Crossref API before it was
  # written down - title, authors, venue, year and DOI are verified, not recalled. See
  # docs/audit.md A5, which is the finding this practice answers.
  - type: article
    title: "Estimating cumulative point prevalence of rare diseases: analysis of the Orphanet database"
    authors:
      - { family-names: "Nguengang Wakap", given-names: Stephanie }
    doi: "10.1038/s41431-019-0508-0"
    journal: "European Journal of Human Genetics"
    volume: 28
    start: 165
    end: 173
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      The field's reference prevalence estimate, and the source of the shape
      rare-disease-scale.md §0 is built on: 3.5-5.9% of the population (263-446 million people),
      71.9% genetic, 69.9% exclusively paediatric, and - the load-bearing part - 84.5% of
      analysable diseases below 1/1,000,000 while 77.3-80.7% of the population burden sits in the
      4.2% (n=149) at the common end. It is also the external check on our own atlas: Orphanet's
      6,172 diseases against the 14,831 our join reaches, which is a difference in scope rather
      than a contradiction.
  - type: article
    title: "The mutational constraint spectrum quantified from variation in 141,456 humans"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Karczewski, given-names: "Konrad J." }
      - { family-names: Francioli, given-names: "Laurent C." }
    doi: "10.1038/s41586-020-2308-7"
    journal: "Nature"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      gnomAD constraint, cited for a Stage 6 claim and a Stage 3 caveat at once. It is the best
      available structural prior for entities with no literature (rare-disease-scale.md §4), and
      its panel composition is exactly why rare-disease-equity.md §2 requires a constraint-based
      prior to carry its population in the manifest, the way null_blocks is carried.
  - type: article
    title: "The Human Phenotype Ontology in 2021"
    authors:
      - { family-names: "Kohler", given-names: Sebastian }
      - { family-names: Gargano, given-names: Michael }
    doi: "10.1093/nar/gkaa1043"
    journal: "Nucleic Acids Research"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      The annotation source every rare-disease layer here is joined on - tools/build_atlas.py,
      tools/dossier.py, tools/nongene_measure.py. Cited for what it CANNOT do as much as for what
      it can: its inheritance vocabulary has no term for a molecule at a dose, an antibody clone
      or a pathogen at eight weeks, which is the measured zero behind rare-disease-mechanisms.md
      §3 - six of ten authored non-gene classes have a footprint of exactly zero.
  - type: article
    title: "100,000 Genomes Pilot on Rare-Disease Diagnosis in Health Care - Preliminary Report"
    doi: "10.1056/NEJMoa2035790"
    journal: "New England Journal of Medicine"
    year: 2021
    notes: >-
      The honest denominator for anything downstream of diagnosis: a 25% diagnostic yield in the
      best-resourced programme available, 14% of those found outside standard panel regions.
      Supports rare-disease-equity.md §2.

  # --- rare-disease mechanisms: the modules (docs/references/rare-disease-mechanisms.md) ---
  - type: article
    title: "The RASopathies"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Rauen, given-names: "Katherine A." }
    doi: "10.1146/annurev-genom-091212-153523"
    journal: "Annual Review of Genomics and Human Genetics"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Defines the RAS-MAPK module of rare-disease-mechanisms.md §1, and the claim that membership
      rather than gene identity is what makes a pathway inhibitor a cross-syndrome candidate.
  - type: article
    title: "The mTOR signalling cascade: paving new roads to cure neurological disease"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Crino, given-names: "Peter B." }
    doi: "10.1038/nrneurol.2016.81"
    journal: "Nature Reviews Neurology"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      The mTORopathies, and the module where somatic mosaicism is the rule rather than the
      exception - which is also the one non-gene class HPO can partly record (136 diseases under
      mosaic terms, tools/nongene_measure.py).
  - type: article
    title: "Genes and molecular pathways underpinning ciliopathies"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Reiter, given-names: "Jeremy F." }
      - { family-names: Leroux, given-names: "Michel R." }
    doi: "10.1038/nrm.2017.60"
    journal: "Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology"
    year: 2017
    notes: >-
      The cleanest case for the module claim: one organelle explains a phenotype set (retina,
      kidney, cerebellum, digits) that shares no organ system. rare-disease-mechanisms.md §1.
  - type: article
    title: "Ribosomopathies: human disorders of ribosome dysfunction"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Narla, given-names: Anupama }
      - { family-names: Ebert, given-names: "Benjamin L." }
    doi: "10.1182/blood-2009-10-178129"
    journal: "Blood"
    year: 2010
    notes: >-
      Cited for the paradox the module framing has to survive: a defect in a universal
      housekeeping machine producing strictly tissue-restricted disease. That is the same
      gene-in-a-context claim this repository's core statistic rests on.
  - type: article
    title: "Lysosomal storage diseases"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Platt, given-names: "Frances M." }
      - { family-names: "d Azzo", given-names: Alessandra }
    doi: "10.1038/s41572-018-0025-4"
    journal: "Nature Reviews Disease Primers"
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      The therapeutic template of the rare-disease field - enzyme replacement, substrate
      reduction, chaperones - and the module where "membership buys you a candidate" is best
      evidenced.
  - type: article
    title: "Mitochondrial diseases"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Gorman, given-names: "Grainne S." }
      - { family-names: Chinnery, given-names: "Patrick F." }
    doi: "10.1038/nrdp.2016.80"
    journal: "Nature Reviews Disease Primers"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      Two genomes and heteroplasmy: the module where inheritance is quantitative rather than
      presence/absence. It is the standing counter-example to any gene-shaped architecture,
      including this repository's own (rare-disease-mechanisms.md §1).
  - type: article
    title: "The human disease network"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Goh, given-names: "Kwang-Il" }
      - { family-names: Cusick, given-names: "Michael E." }
    doi: "10.1073/pnas.0701361104"
    journal: "Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences"
    year: 2007
    notes: >-
      The bipartite disease-gene graph, and the first demonstration that disease genes cluster
      rather than scatter. It is the ancestor of the object tools/interactome_sparse.py measured
      for an entirely different (computational) reason.
  - type: article
    title: "Uncovering disease-disease relationships through the incomplete interactome"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Menche, given-names: "Jorg" }
      - { family-names: Sharma, given-names: Amitabh }
    doi: "10.1126/science.1257601"
    journal: "Science"
    year: 2015
    notes: >-
      Disease modules, and network distance between them as a predictor of shared phenotype. Our
      measurement is adjacent rather than confirmatory: modularity 0.861 on the real HPO graph
      against 0.162 for a degree-matched rewiring. Same object, different statistic, no interval -
      and rare-disease-mechanisms.md §5 states the null that would falsify it.
  - type: article
    title: "Patient-Customized Oligonucleotide Therapy for a Rare Genetic Disease"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Kim, given-names: Jinkuk }
    doi: "10.1056/NEJMoa1813279"
    journal: "New England Journal of Medicine"
    year: 2019
    notes: >-
      milasen: the n=1 limiting case of the power axis. Cited in rare-disease-scale.md §4 for what
      it implies about this library's scope - at n=1 no calibration helps and the whole
      methodological weight moves onto mechanism. It marks the edge of where Stage 1 applies.

  # --- rare-disease equity: the social layer (docs/references/rare-disease-equity.md) ------
  - type: article
    title: "Time to diagnosis and determinants of diagnostic delays of people living with a rare disease: results of a Rare Barometer retrospective patient survey"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Faye, given-names: Francois }
      - { family-names: Crocione, given-names: Claudia }
    doi: "10.1038/s41431-024-01604-z"
    journal: "European Journal of Human Genetics"
    year: 2024
    notes: >-
      6,507 people, 1,675 rare diseases, 41 countries; average total diagnosis time 4.7 years, and
      56% diagnosed more than six months after first medical contact. Usable here rather than
      merely quotable because its determinants carry intervals: adolescence OR 4.79 (3.7-6.2),
      childhood 3.11 (2.4-4.0), misdiagnosis 2.48 (2.1-2.9), being a woman 1.22 (1.1-1.4). This is
      the mechanism by which a social fact becomes the annotation-count attention proxy that
      tools/atlas_bias.py measures at +0.2357.
  - type: article
    title: "The Missing Diversity in Human Genetic Studies"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Sirugo, given-names: Giorgio }
      - { family-names: Williams, given-names: "Scott M." }
      - { family-names: Tishkoff, given-names: "Sarah A." }
    doi: "10.1016/j.cell.2019.02.048"
    journal: "Cell"
    year: 2019
    notes: >-
      With Popejoy and Fullerton, the documented composition of the reference panels any Stage 6
      prior here would be built on. The consequence is specific rather than rhetorical:
      under-represented ancestry yields more apparently rare variants, more variants of uncertain
      significance, and a lower diagnostic yield.
  - type: article
    title: "Genomics is failing on diversity"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Popejoy, given-names: "Alice B." }
      - { family-names: Fullerton, given-names: "Stephanie M." }
    doi: "10.1038/538161a"
    journal: "Nature"
    year: 2016
    notes: >-
      The measurement that made panel composition a citable fact rather than an impression.
      Supports rare-disease-equity.md §2.
  - type: article
    title: "The RDI-Lancet Commission on Rare Diseases: improving visibility to address health-care disparities for 400 million people"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Boycott, given-names: "Kym M." }
      - { family-names: Giugliani, given-names: Roberto }
    doi: "10.1016/S0140-6736(25)00211-9"
    journal: "The Lancet"
    year: 2025
    notes: >-
      The policy-scale framing of the same gap, at a population count (400 million) consistent
      with the 263-446 million interval of Nguengang Wakap et al. Supports rare-disease-equity.md
      §4, where the barriers layer's economic claim is contradicted by this project's own
      arithmetic and the contradiction is published rather than reconciled.

  # --- ancestry, founder history and geography (docs/references/rare-disease-ancestry.md) --
  # Crossref-verified, like the three blocks above. Several DOIs guessed from memory during
  # drafting came back 404 or resolved to an unrelated paper; those were discarded rather than
  # written down. That is the practice docs/audit.md F2 proposes to automate.
  - type: article
    title: "Finnish Disease Heritage I: characteristics, causes, background"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Norio, given-names: Reijo }
    doi: "10.1007/s00439-002-0875-3"
    journal: "Human Genetics"
    year: 2003
    notes: >-
      The founding description of a founder-disease heritage: ~36 autosomal-recessive disorders
      enriched by repeated bottlenecks, most rare or absent elsewhere - and, symmetrically, cystic
      fibrosis rare in Finland. Supports rare-disease-ancestry.md §2a, and it is the reason our
      own §1c spread is read as demography rather than as pathology.
  - type: article
    title: "The Finnish genetic heritage in 2022 - from diagnosis to translational research"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Uusimaa, given-names: Johanna }
      - { family-names: Kettunen, given-names: Johannes }
      - { family-names: Varilo, given-names: Teppo }
    doi: "10.1242/dmm.049490"
    journal: "Disease Models & Mechanisms"
    year: 2022
    notes: "The modern update to Norio: what a well-characterised founder population yields once it is sequenced. Supports rare-disease-ancestry.md §2a."
  - type: article
    title: "Human Genetics: Lessons from Quebec Populations"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Scriver, given-names: "Charles R." }
    doi: "10.1146/annurev.genom.2.1.69"
    journal: "Annual Review of Genomics and Human Genetics"
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      A founder population from roughly 8,500 settlers, with regional sub-founder effects
      producing 30+ enriched Mendelian conditions. Cited for the mechanism, and for the point our
      measurement cannot make: sub-population structure is invisible to a country-level field.
  - type: article
    title: "The population genetics of the Jewish people"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Ostrer, given-names: Harry }
      - { family-names: Skorecki, given-names: Karl }
    doi: "10.1007/s00439-012-1235-6"
    journal: "Human Genetics"
    year: 2012
    notes: >-
      The population structure behind the disorders our measurement found hiding inside an untyped
      string: Canavan disease, mucolipidosis II and IV and cystinosis are tagged 'Specific
      population' with no population named (rare-disease-ancestry.md §1d).
  - type: article
    title: "Carnitine levels in 26,462 individuals from the nationwide screening program for primary carnitine deficiency in the Faroe Islands"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Rasmussen, given-names: Jan }
      - { family-names: Nielsen, given-names: "Olav W." }
      - { family-names: Janzen, given-names: Nils }
    doi: "10.1007/s10545-013-9606-2"
    journal: "Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      The source of the most extreme row our own measurement surfaced: Faroe Islands 6-9/10 000
      and >1/1000 for systemic primary carnitine deficiency against <1/1 000 000 in China. It also
      makes the confound concrete - that extreme came from a deliberate nationwide screen of
      26,462 people, so it is a founder effect AND a measurement artefact at once, which is
      exactly what tools/ancestry_geography.py reports it cannot separate.
  - type: article
    title: "Consanguinity, human evolution, and complex diseases"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Bittles, given-names: "Alan H." }
      - { family-names: Black, given-names: "Michael L." }
    doi: "10.1073/pnas.0906079106"
    journal: "Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences"
    year: 2010
    notes: >-
      Couples related as second cousins or closer, and their children, at about 10.4% of the
      global population. The mechanism behind the Saudi Arabia row in rare-disease-ancestry.md
      §1c, and behind the point that consanguineous populations are exceptionally INFORMATIVE for
      recessive gene discovery - the opposite of the deficit framing.
  - type: article
    title: "Global epidemiology of sickle haemoglobin in neonates: a contemporary geostatistical model-based map and population estimates"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Piel, given-names: "Frédéric B." }
      - { family-names: Patil, given-names: "Anand P." }
      - { family-names: Howes, given-names: "Rosalind E." }
    doi: "10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61229-X"
    journal: "The Lancet"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Balancing selection as the third enrichment mechanism, distinct from drift and consanguinity:
      an allele common because it was useful. Cited for the consequence to a screen - the standard
      'too common to be causal' frequency filter has a different threshold depending on whose
      genome is on the bench (rare-disease-ancestry.md §2c).
  - type: article
    title: "The Genetic Structure and History of Africans and African Americans"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Tishkoff, given-names: "Sarah A." }
      - { family-names: Reed, given-names: "Floyd A." }
      - { family-names: Friedlaender, given-names: "Françoise R." }
    doi: "10.1126/science.1172257"
    journal: "Science"
    year: 2009
    notes: >-
      The necessary counterweight to every founder-population citation above: African populations
      carry more genetic diversity than all non-African populations combined, so founder logic does
      not transfer and 'Africa' is not a population. It is what makes our measured representation
      ratio of 0.07 a statement about the least-described and most-diverse part of human variation
      rather than about a small gap in a small place.
  - type: article
    title: "Improved allele frequencies in gnomAD through local ancestry inference"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Kore, given-names: Shadi }
      - { family-names: Wilson, given-names: Michael }
      - { family-names: Tiao, given-names: Grace }
    doi: "10.1038/s41467-025-63340-2"
    journal: "Nature Communications"
    year: 2025
    notes: >-
      The number that turns 'panels are skewed' into a magnitude a pipeline can act on: across 27
      million variants in two admixed groups, 78.5% and 85.1% show at least a TWOFOLD difference in
      ancestry-specific frequency. A single pooled frequency is the wrong number for most variants
      in an admixed genome. Supports rare-disease-ancestry.md §3 and ADR 0005.
  - type: article
    title: "Clinical use of current polygenic risk scores may exacerbate health disparities"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Martin, given-names: "Alicia R." }
      - { family-names: Kanai, given-names: Masahiro }
      - { family-names: Kamatani, given-names: Yoichiro }
    doi: "10.1038/s41588-019-0379-x"
    journal: "Nature Genetics"
    year: 2019
    notes: "The reference statement on score non-transferability, and the reason a Stage 6 prior must carry its panel composition rather than be assumed portable."
  - type: article
    title: "The CARE Principles for Indigenous Data Governance"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Carroll, given-names: "Stephanie Russo" }
      - { family-names: Garba, given-names: Ibrahim }
      - { family-names: "Figueroa-Rodríguez", given-names: "Oscar L." }
    doi: "10.5334/dsj-2020-043"
    journal: "Data Science Journal"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      Collective benefit, Authority to control, Responsibility, Ethics. Cited as a CONFORMANCE GAP
      against this repository: references/standards.md lists FAIR, which governs data movement and
      is silent on who a dataset is about. ADR 0005 proposes adding CARE beside it with the status
      set honestly to 'not applicable to current inputs, and not designed for'.
  - type: article
    title: "Rights, interests and expectations: Indigenous perspectives on unrestricted access to genomic data"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Hudson, given-names: Maui }
      - { family-names: Garrison, given-names: "Nanibaa' A." }
      - { family-names: Sterling, given-names: Rogena }
    doi: "10.1038/s41576-020-0228-x"
    journal: "Nature Reviews Genetics"
    year: 2020
    notes: "States the case against unrestricted access as an unqualified good - the direct counterweight to the FAIR reflex in a repository that has one. Supports rare-disease-ancestry.md §5."
  - type: article
    title: "Genetic disease risks of under-represented founder populations in New York City"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Isshiki, given-names: Mariko }
      - { family-names: Griffen, given-names: Jonathan }
      - { family-names: Meissner, given-names: Paul }
    doi: "10.1371/journal.pgen.1011755"
    journal: "PLOS Genetics"
    year: 2025
    notes: >-
      The current-decade version of the founder-population argument, and the one that undercuts the
      geographic framing our own measurement is built on: founder structure travels with people,
      so a country-level field cannot see a founder community inside a large diverse city. Named in
      rare-disease-ancestry.md §2a as a limit on §1.

  # --- visualisation forms ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # Each supports a form built in web/src/components/viz/. Genealogy and what our numbers do
  # to each claim: docs/lineage.md §14; the form-by-form argument:
  # docs/references/visualization-canon.md §7d.
  - type: article
    title: "Scatterplot matrix techniques for large N"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Carr, given-names: "Daniel B." }
      - { family-names: Littlefield, given-names: "Richard J." }
      - { family-names: Nicholson, given-names: "Wesley L." }
      - { family-names: Littlefield, given-names: "John S." }
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: "82"
    year: 1987
    notes: >-
      Hexagonal binning. Supports the selectivity plane's density encoding, which replaced an
      800-point overplotted scatter and showed the plane is bimodal - a structure alpha blending
      had hidden. Hexagons over squares because a hexagon's centre is equidistant from all six
      neighbours, so binning does not band along an axis.
  - type: article
    title: "Raincloud plots: a multi-platform tool for robust data visualization"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Allen, given-names: Micah }
      - { family-names: Poggiali, given-names: Davide }
      - { family-names: Whitaker, given-names: Kirstie }
      - { family-names: Marshall, given-names: "Tom Rhys" }
      - { family-names: Kievit, given-names: "Rogier A." }
    journal: "Wellcome Open Research"
    volume: "4"
    year: 2019
    notes: >-
      Density plus summary plus every observation. Supports replacing the populations box plot,
      which drew the candidate class as one wide box where the raincloud shows two modes
      (z about -1.3 and about +15). A five-number summary cannot express that, and the second
      mode is the most interesting thing on that panel.
  - type: article
    title: "UpSet: visualization of intersecting sets"
    authors:
      - { family-names: Lex, given-names: Alexander }
      - { family-names: Gehlenborg, given-names: Nils }
      - { family-names: Vuillemot, given-names: Romain }
      - { family-names: Streit, given-names: Marc }
      - { family-names: Pfister, given-names: Hanspeter }
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics"
    volume: "20"
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      Intersections on a common baseline instead of areas in a Venn. Supports two figures: the
      run's flag overlap (64 genes are raw-top-100, calibrated-top-100 and common-essential at
      once) and the catalogue's gap patterns (1,326 of 8,574 diseases are missing gene, onset
      and sign denominators together). Before these, nothing here could show that an entity
      carries two labels at once.

